  "text": "umably, the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AD}$ intersects $\\overline{AD}$ at its midpoint. Let's assume point $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{AD}.$ We will call the sides opposite the trapezoid $AD$ and $BC$ as parallel bases of the trapezoid, with the lengths of these bases being $a = 3$ and $b = 6,$ respectively. The height (the perpendicular distance between the bases) that we'll call $h,$ and the lengths of $\\overline{AD}$ and $\\overline{BC},$ respectively, as $d$ and $c.$\n\nFirst, we draw a descending perpendicular from $B$ of the longer base $AD$ to the shorter base $BC.$ Let's call the intersection point $E.$\n\nUsing the cosine rule in $\\triangle ABE$ and the definitions of the sides, we identify:\n\\[\n\\cos \\angle A = \\frac{AE}{\\overline{AD}} = \\frac{AE}{d}\n\\]\nThis implies that\n\\[\n\\cos \\angle A = \\frac{AE}{d} = \\frac{1}{8}.\n\\]\n\nSince $AE$ is perpendicular and cancels out in a right triangle, we discern that\n\\[\nd = 3 + 4x,\n\\]\n\nwith the same coefficient for both bases to solve the trapezoid symmetrization, assuming proportional triangles.\n\nSetting $AE$ equal to $\\overline{\\overline{AE}}$ (a result from the height scaling), we have\n\\[\n\\text{AE} = h(\\overline{AB}) = \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot3h.\n\\]\n\nHence,\n\\[\n\\left( 3+\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot3h \\right)/2 = \\frac{d}{2},\n\\]\nwhich gives\n\\[\n\\frac{16}{8} \\cdot 3 + \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 3h = 4d.\n\\]\n\nThus, we ultralyze this yields\n\\[\n6 + \\frac{3h}{8} = d + \\frac{d}{8}.\n\\]\n\nExhibiting algebraically:\n\nExpanding further, \n\\[\n6 + \\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{h}{8} = d \\rightarrow \\boxed{8}.\n\nSince it is evaluated thus,\nSince dividing by eight formulating in units relations the defined harmonic $8$ confirms integer division the calculated length geometricalally equals more accurate as per metrics:\n\n\\boxed{4}",
